### `Prefer: count=exact` fills the page envelope's `count` with the size of the MATCHING SET, not of the
### page — seven work orders are visible here and the page holds two, so a count that was secretly the
### page's own row count is a different number.
# @name CountExact
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=2&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Prefer: count=exact

### The count is narrowed by the caller's own filter, exactly as the rows are
# @name CountFiltered
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=2&order=reference.asc&status=eq.scheduled
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Prefer: count=exact

### The same filter without the preference, so "the filter narrows the count" is compared with something
# @name FilteredWithoutCount
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc&status=eq.scheduled
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### The count does not shrink as the walk advances: page two of the same query reports the same total
# @name CountOnPageTwo
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=2&order=reference.asc&after={{CountExact.response.body.$.next}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Prefer: count=exact

### `planned` and `estimated` degrade to a real count — a planner estimate exists on one supported engine
### and not the other — and `Preference-Applied` tells the caller what was actually done
# @name CountPlanned
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=2&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Prefer: count=planned

### Opt-in: a request that sends no preference gets `count: null` — PRESENT and null, never absent
# @name NoCountAsked
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=2&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### RFC 7240 §2: a preference the server does not recognise is IGNORED, not refused — the one deliberate
### departure from this API's "refuse, never ignore" rule, and the absence of `Preference-Applied` is how
### the standard says it is reported
# @name UnrecognisedPreference
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=2&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Prefer: count=exakt

### The count is over the POLICY-FILTERED set: a technician sees only the work orders assigned to them, so
### their count is their own subset and never the seven rows the dispatcher can see
# @name CountAsTechnician
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyTechNorth}}
Prefer: count=exact
